{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793042086,"nonce":"4C7g6EZgEjgvTG9GM8kvWjAlS3o=","message":"Completed in 3306295.\n\nNative hello advertises bufferedRestore:1. The trusted control plane can restore a complete CRDT checkpoint with mode:\"buffer\" and the expected saved-file SHA-256. The existing UTF-8 file remains unchanged while the recovered graph is stored in one versioned, checksummed private record. Background flushes, last-subscriber release and shutdown persist this graph without writing its text to disk. Reopen/restart verifies the baseline and preserves graph identity. Recovered buffers continue requiring explicit Save.\n\nExplicit Save checks the observed disk baseline and writes an intent containing the graph plus old/new disk hashes before writing the file. Reopening accepts either side of that interrupted Save; unrelated outside changes cause recovery-conflict and preserve both versions. Direct exact-path writes cannot overwrite dirty recovered buffers, even after their live document is released. Clean binary/text replacement retains the earlier graph. Corrupt/incomplete private recovery now fails closed rather than being silently reseeded. Legacy raw graphs and replace-mode restoration remain supported.\n\nDocument open reports buffered/dirty. Browser projection and Haxe binding propagate restored dirtiness even when browser recovery already has identical text. Browser-forwarded restore remains denied, and existing native authorization checks still apply.\n\nValidation: 53 native, 39 browser-session and 18 Worker tests passed, plus SessionEditorChecks. Native fault tests cover failure before the Save intent, failure of the disk write, and failure after the disk write. Coverage also includes Unicode, flush/release/shutdown/restart, changed/missing/non-text baselines, active aliases, corruption, dirty direct-write refusal and clean file-type replacement retaining graph history. The hosted test deletes all compute files/state, reconstructs a saved file from durable operation bytes and overlays its unsaved journal, then verifies a second restart and explicit Save. The final native history adjustment passed the targeted suite again and the binary was rebuilt.\n\nBoth full-WASM flows passed: collaboration/undo/reconnect/recovery and the hosted GitHub flow with buffered recovery. The latter checks that a recovered tab is dirty and saveable before further typing, later edits remain off disk, durable Save succeeds, and reload works. Core-only check, WASM/native builds and diff checks passed. Tested bundle staged at bin/web/rayde; local session refreshed with URL, state and credentials preserved; assets/capability/file read checked.\n\nScope remains the restoration seam, not automatic VM recovery. A complete filesystem/Git manifest, private-state lifecycle for file/directory moves and deletions, stable identities, external-write fencing/reconciliation, compute leases and MSB provisioning remain open. Baseline checks are not a filesystem compare-and-swap. Private graph payload is bounded to 4 MiB with a bounded header. Local tests use emulated Cloudflare resources; real integration is not yet required. No push or deployment.","files":null}]}